      "final_text": "Done. All five requirements are proved and the result reproduces.\n\n**Fix** ([`candidate/Solution.lean`](<workspace>/candidate/Solution.lean)): `VyperSqrt.sqrt` is now the deployed loop with the PR-4486 correction \u2014 the seed `x / 2.0 + 0.5`, at most 256 Newton iterations stopping on equal successive iterates, returning the smaller of the last two iterates (`if y < z then y else z`). This rounds the root down instead of sometimes up.\n\n**Proofs**: the two easy requirements are direct (`req_sqrt_neg_reverts`, `req_deployed_loop`). The hard part shows the loop computes the integer floor root on every positive decimal: the Nat Newton sequence from the seed stays positive, above `Nat.sqrt (a\u00b710\u00b9\u2070)`, and in decimal range; the seed is at least the root; the halving bound brings the error to \u2264 1 within 169 steps and `min_newton_eq_sqrt` closes the endgame, so the final pair's minimum is exactly the root (early loop exit is a fixed point, hence also the root). The Int-level bounds then follow from `Nat.sqrt_le` / `Nat.lt_succ_sqrt`, giving `req_sqrt_not_above`, `req_sqrt_not_below`, and `req_sqrt_total`. No `sorry`, no forbidden constructs; only allowed imports.\n\n**Verification**:\n- `lake build Solution`: compiles clean.\n- `check_candidate`: `\"status\": \"VERIFIED\"` \u2014 all 5 requirements VERIFIED, accepted by all six kernels, axioms limited to `propext`/`Quot.sound`/`Classical.choice`, executable property tests PASS (2001 cases, 0 violations).\n- `verify_passport`: `reproduced` \u2014 Passport `.proofforge/passport-001.json` (`f9e2e15b\u20269318ce`).",
